|}</cleantable>{{Quote Box|''You do not even know our tongue, do you? Such arrogance, to dare take for yourself the name of Dovah.''|Alduin speaking to the [[Lore:Last Dragonborn|Last Dragonborn]]{{ref|name=A}} (listen <ab>SR-audio-Alduin Speaking to Dragonborn.ogg</ab>)}}</noinclude>
[[Lore:Alduin|Alduin]] (also spelled '''Ald'uin'''{{ref|name=500COTOYTR|group=UOL|}} or simply '''Ald'''),{{Ref|name=ALAS|[[ON:Alduin Living Armor Senche|Alduin Living Armor Senche]] mount description in [[ON:Online|ESO]]}}{{Ref|name=SSOS|group=UOL|{{Cite Book|ns_base=GEN|Shor Son of Shor}}}} known as the '''World-Eater''',{{ref|name=VOF|}} the '''First Dragon''' (or '''First of Dragons'''),{{ref|name=TSoG|}}{{Ref|name=ALAS}} the '''Dragon Who Eats the World''',{{Ref|name=APQ|}} '''Time-Eater''',{{ref|name=FSOKW|}} the '''Twilight God''',{{ref|name=DATN|}} the '''Worm''',{{ref|name=SRTsun|[[Skyrim:Tsun|Tsun]]'s dialogue in ''[[Skyrim:Skyrim|Skyrim]]''}} the '''Dragon King''',{{ref|Official [[Online:Online|ESO]] website: [[General:Ask Us Anything: Gathering and Exploration|Ask Us Anything: Gathering and Exploration]]}} and the '''Firstborn'''{{ref|name=SI|}} is an immensely powerful black [[Lore:Dragon|Dragon]]. He is known as a malevolent being who destroys the world periodically, and the [[Lore:Nord|Nords]] believe that his reappearance heralds the end of the current [[Lore:Kalpa|kalpa]] and the beginning of the next.{{ref|name=AIR|{{Cite Book|Alduin is Real}}}} He is the self-proclaimed First-Born of [[Lore:Akatosh|Akatosh]],{{ref|name=A|}}{{ref|name=Paarthurnax|[[Lore:Paarthurnax|Paarthurnax]]'s dialogue in ''[[Skyrim:Skyrim|Skyrim]]''}} and supposedly an aspect of him,{{ref|name=7F|group=UOL|{{Cite Book|The Seven Fights of The Aldudagga|ns_base=GEN}}}} having also been described as a "fragment of Akatosh's soul".{{ref|name=VonosJournal}} Alduin is said to have begun as "the wisest and most far-seeing" of all Dragons.{{ref|name=Paarthurnax|}}

His name can be separated into "Al Du In", which means "Destroyer Devour Master" in the [[Lore:Dragon Language|Dragon Language]].{{ref|name=Prima|{{Cite Book|ns_base=Books|The Elder Scrolls V: Skyrim: Prima Official Game Guide}}}}

Alduin once ruled dragonkind as their king, and later extended his rule over mankind through the [[Lore:Dragon Cult|Dragon Cult]], being worshiped as a deity during the [[Lore:Merethic Era|Merethic Era]]. Towards the end of the [[Lore:Dragon War|Dragon War]], Alduin was banished by the Nords through the use of an [[Lore:Elder Scrolls|Elder Scroll]], the creation of the dragon-crippling [[Lore:Thu'um|Thu'um]] Dragonrend, and the assistance of [[Lore:Kyne|Kyne]] and [[Lore:Paarthurnax|Paarthurnax]], and sent into the far future.{{ref|name=SkyrimAB}} Worship of Alduin dwindled following his banishment, shifting towards recognition as a god that held great respect and fear.{{ref|name=TSoG}} The Nords' view of Alduin has changed drastically over various points of history, and his exact relationship to Akatosh has been debated by [[Lore:Imperial|Imperials]] and Nords alike.{{ref|name=DATN|}}{{ref|name=AIR|}}{{ref|name=TAAD|}} Worship of Akatosh eventually overtook Alduin's place in the Nordic pantheon. In the [[Lore:Fourth Era|Fourth Era]], Alduin returned from his banishment and set about reviving his fallen brothers, beginning the [[Lore:Return of the Dragons|Return of the Dragons]].<noinclude>
{{NewLeft}}

==History==
[[File:SR-concept-Alduin.jpg|The First Dragon|thumb|left]][[File:ON-texture-Dragonclash.png|Dragonclash, a symbol depicting a clash of Alduin and Akatosh|thumb|right]]
===Origins===
Alduin and his various aspects (such as the [[Lore:Skaal|Skaal]] [[Lore:Thartaag|Thartaag]] or the Khajiiti [[Lore:Alkhan|Alkhan]]) serve as the [[Lore:Dragon|Dragon]] God of the End Times. Though some mortal sources suggest that Alduin is a Nordic cultural equivalent to the [[Lore:Akatosh|Time Dragon]] given his nature as a Dragon and his relation to time,{{ref|name=DATN|{{Cite Book|Divines and the Nords}}}}{{ref|name=VOF|{{Cite Book|Varieties of Faith...}}}}{{ref|name=TTOD|{{Cite Book|The Tale of Dro'Zira}}}} others suggest a more complicated lineage.

Alduin describes himself as "firstborn of Akatosh",{{ref|name=A|[[Skyrim:Alduin|Alduin]]'s dialogue in ''[[Skyrim:Skyrim|Skyrim]]''}} with other sources (both mortal and draconic) corroborating this claim.{{ref|name=Paarthurnax|}}{{ref|name=DC|{{Cite Book|Temples of the Dragon Cult}}}}{{ref|name=VonosJournal|{{Cite Book|Vonos' Journal}}}} Some sources make no reference to the father-son relationship between Alduin and Akatosh, but do make a point to distinguish the two from one another.{{ref|name=AIR|}}{{ref|name=ONAT|[[Online:Apprentice Taz|Apprentice Taz]]'s dialogue in [[Online:Online|ESO]]: ''[[Online:Elsweyr|Elsweyr]]''}}{{ref|name=TAAD|{{Cite Book|The Alduin/Akatosh Dichotomy}}}}{{Ref|name=APQ|{{Cite Book|Artorius Ponticus Answers Your Questions}}}} Other parts of Nord folklore describe Alduin as having been shed from Aka-Tusk, an elder dragon totem from the time of the [[Lore:Ehlnofey|Ehlnofey]].{{ref|name=7F|group=UOL|}}{{Ref|name=MK|group=UOL|{{Cite Book|Michael Kirkbride AMA|ns_base=GEN}}}} One Nordic myth describes "Ald, son of Ald" as the succeeding iteration of the Time Dragon in the current kalpa, detailing a process that occurs each cycle where the previous kalpa's "Ald, father of Ald" and "[[Lore:Shor|Shor, father of Shor]]" are replaced by their respective sons, who ignore their fathers' wisdom and go to war again despite already knowing the outcome.{{Ref|name=SSOS|group=UOL}} All dragons are said to be in some manner related to Akatosh, being his children and/or part of him that split off when time began.{{Ref|name=SI}} Similarly, Pre-Riddle'thar [[Lore:Khajiit|Khajiiti]] mythology remembers Alkhan as the firstborn offspring of [[Lore:Akha|Akha]] and a mysterious entity only known as a "[[Lore:Demon of Fire and Shadow|demon of fire and shadow]]".{{Ref|name=TWaS|}} While Akha is linked to the Khajiiti Alkosh, they are at least partially distinct figures.{{Ref|name=TWaS|}}

===Dawn Era===
Alduin is ascribed the creation of the World in certain Nordic mythology, by way of destroying the prior world to begin the current one in the form of a great firestorm. While not chief, for this act of creation Alduin is considered the wellspring, the origin, of the Nordic pantheon.{{ref|name=VOF|}} At least one Nordic folklore tale speaks to one of these World Eating events. In a previous [[Lore: Kalpa|iteration of the world]], Alduin was subject to a scheme by [[Lore: The Greedy Man|The Greedy Man]] and the Demon King [[Lore: Mehrunes Dagon|Dagon]], who sought to trap Alduin in a dying world and kill him by tricking him into consuming more than he was able. This scheme was unsuccessful, however, and resulted in Dagon's transformation into the Prince of Destruction.{{ref|name=7F|group=UOL|}}

The World-Eater was said to have participated in key battles during the [[Lore:Dawn Era|Dawn Era]] with [[Lore:Lorkhan|Lorkhan]] and his various cultural interpretations or aspects.{{Ref|name=TWaS|}}{{ref|name=FSOKW|{{Cite Book|Five Songs of King Wulfharth}}}} In the Khajiiti interpretation, Alkhan was said to have been killed by Lorkhaj and his companions during the early days of creation but per his immortal nature as a son of Akha, it was thought he'd eventually return from the [[Lore:Many Paths|Many Paths]] in time.{{Ref|name=TWaS|}} In one Nordic interpretation, Alduin led the Moot at the [[Lore:Adamantine Tower|House of We]] where Shor was judged for his crimes in war and sentenced to death.{{ref|name=SSOS|group=UOL}}

Dragons had existed since the Dawn of time, but they were "wild" and "uncivilized" creatures.{{ref|name=SI|{{Cite Book|Shalidor's Insights}}}} Alduin returned to the affairs of the world and created their civilization, establishing order among the dragons, and becoming their leader.{{ref|name=SI}} It is said the Dragons ruled over all of Mundus before the birth of men.{{ref|name=Hrothgar|[[Skyrim:High Hrothgar#Etched Tablets|Etched Tablets]] of High Hrothgar in ''[[Skyrim:Skyrim|Skyrim]]''}}

===Dragon Cult===
[[File:MER-art-Skyrim Dragons.webp|Lord Alduin and his brother Paarthurnax|thumb|left]][[File:SR-concept-Dragon Mural.jpg|Dragon Cult Alduin mural|thumb|right]]
This leadership over dragonkind gradually extended to rulership over [[Lore:Human|mankind]]. This [[Lore:Dragon Cult|Dragon Cult]] originated in [[Lore:Atmora|Atmora]] but would eventually come south to [[Lore:Tamriel|Tamriel]] where the humans were lorded over with a much crueler hand.{{ref|name=TDW|{{Cite Book|The Dragon War}}}} Beyond the more historically documented rule in Tamriel and Atmora, the Dragons at the height of their power were said to rule the whole [[Lore:Nirn|world]].{{ref|name=TGATT|}} Though all dragons swore fealty to Alduin,{{ref|name=Arngeir|[[Skyrim:Arngeir|Arngeir]]'s dialogue in ''[[Skyrim:Skyrim|Skyrim]]''}} his younger brother, [[Lore:Paarthurnax|Paarthurnax]], served as his chief lieutenant and right hand.{{Ref|name=SREsbern|[[SR:Esbern|Esbern]]'s dialogue in ''[[Skyrim:Skyrim|Skyrim]]''}} Others like [[Lore:Kaalgrontiid|Kaalgrontiid]] traveled further south, seeking to establish their own kingdoms outside of Alduin's direct supervision.{{Ref|name=LADragons|{{Cite Book|Loremaster's Archive - Dragons in the Second Era}}}} Under the dragons were certain humans who held a special connection with the dragons, and known as the [[Lore:Dragon Priest|Dragon Priests]].{{ref|name=TDW|}} At the bottom rung lay the mortal servants who lived under the heel of these cruel masters.{{ref|name=TDW|}} It has been theorised that Alduin administered sacrificial rituals of the cult that might have involved dragons themselves suffering the sacrifice.{{ref|name=RDS|[[Online:Antiquity_Furnishings#Ruby Dragon Skull|Ruby Dragon Skull]] antiquities codex entry in [[Online:Online|ESO]]: ''[[Online:Greymoor|Greymoor]]''}}

[[Lore:Skuldafn|Skuldafn]] was a significant location for the Dragon Cult, and known as one of Alduin's high fanes.{{ref|name=Odahviing}} From here, Alduin could enter a portal that led directly to Sovngarde, allowing him to eat the souls of the Nordic dead, a privilege he guarded jealously.{{ref|name=Odahviing}} The Dragon Priests of Skuldafn (such as [[Lore:Nahkriin|Nahkriin]]) were said to have stood at Alduin's right hand.{{ref|name=EGTSkyrim|{{Cite Book|EGT|Skyrim}}}}

Paarthurnax would eventually have a change of heart.{{ref|name=Arngeir|}} According to the [[Lore:Greybeards|Greybeards]] of High Hrothgar, Paarthurnax began to pity mankind and was called upon by the goddess [[Lore:Kyne|Kyne]] to set things right.{{ref|name=Arngeir|}} Where before the Thu'um had been the sole domain of the Dragons and the chosen few who led the Dragon Cult as priests, Paarthurnax taught it to the mortals of the land in order to give them a means to combat their overlords.{{ref|name=Paarthurnax|}} Some sources claim this was at the command of Akatosh himself.{{ref|name=TDW|}} These humans succeeded in defeating the Dragon Priests and slaughtered the dragons in great number,{{ref|name=TDW|}} but had yet to face their most daunting task of all: confronting the cult's almighty God-King, the World-Eater Alduin.

===Banishment and Looming Return===
[[File:LG-cardart-Cast Into Time.png|Alduin's banishment|thumb|left]]
Three of the greatest [[Lore:Nord|Proto-Nordic]] heroes of the age, [[Lore:Felldir the Old|Felldir the Old]], [[Lore:Hakon One-Eye|Hakon One-Eye]] and [[Lore:Gormlaith Golden-Hilt|Gormlaith Golden-Hilt]], took the task of defeating Alduin upon themselves.{{ref|name=Paarthurnax|}} They initially attempted to recruit the Dragon Priest [[Lore:Miraak|Miraak]] who thanks to his unique nature as the first [[Lore:Dragonborn|Dragonborn]] in history had a tremendous edge against the foe, but Miraak refused to give aid.{{ref|name=Miraak|[[Skyrim:Miraak (person)|Miraak]]'s dialogue in ''[[Skyrim:Skyrim|Skyrim]]: [[Skyrim:Dragonborn|Dragonborn]]''}}

Near the end of the war, these ancient heroes confronted Alduin at the summit of the [[Lore:Throat of the World|Throat of the World]].{{ref|name=SkyrimAB|}} With no conventional means to defeat the wyrm, Felldir the Old used an [[Lore:Elder Scroll|Elder Scroll]] to perform an incantation that ejected Alduin from their time, propelling him into the future and leaving behind a [[Lore:Time Wound|Time Wound]].{{ref|name=SkyrimAB|[[Skyrim:Alduin's Bane|Alduin's Bane]] quest in ''[[Skyrim:Skyrim|Skyrim]]''}}

The legacy of Alduin in Nordic culture following his banishment varied slightly, but was mostly embodied by reverence and fear. Most Nords still regarded Alduin as a [[Lore:Gods|god]], namely the Dragon totem of the old Nordic animal totem religion. As a Twilight God who would usher in the next cycle,{{ref|name=DATN|}} these Nords held great respect and fear for Alduin, but dared not worship him.{{ref|name=TSoG|{{Cite Book|The Song of Gods}}}} Those who did worship him did so in secret, as it was illegal.{{Ref|name=ONContrJ|[[Online:Contraband J#Jewel-Encrusted Dragon Cult Idol|Jewel-Encrusted Dragon Cult Idol]] item description in [[Online:Online|ESO]]}} Peasants spoke of Alduin in hushed tones as they clutched their prayer stones.{{ref|name=ONArlof|[[Online:Arlof|Arlof]]'s dialogue in [[Online:Online|ESO]]: ''[[Online:Greymoor|Greymoor]]''}} Alduin was venerated on [[Lore:Solstice (astronomy)#Winter Solstice|winter solstices]] at ancient Dragon Cult temples where prayers and offerings were made in the hopes of the great god of time continuing his slumber for years to come.{{ref|name=DATN|}}{{ref|group=UOL|name=GTNTR|{{Cite Book|The Nords' Totemic Religion|ns_base=GEN}}}} At winter solstice, Dragon Cultists gather to award their newest initiates specially crafted stone piercings. Some claim Alduin's blood blessed the nose studs, turning them into rubies the shade of his eyes.{{ref|name=ESODCO|[[Online:Dragon Cult Piercings|Dragon Cult Piercings]] item description in [[Online:Online|ESO]]}} The Song of Endings described Alduin as an "appetite with wings", and stated that, whether by flame or by teeth, he consumes all.{{ref|name=ESOWEF|[[Online:World-Eater's Flame|World Eater's Flame]] item description in [[Online:Online|ESO]]}} Some followers of ancient Nord traditions adorn themselves with imagery of a clash between Alduin and Akatosh.{{ref|name=ESODCBM|[[Online:Dragonclash Body Marking|Dragonclash Body Marking]] item description in [[Online:Online|ESO]]}}

[[File:SR-creature-Alduin 03.jpg|Alduin in Sovngarde, the host of several battles between him and Shor|thumb|right]]
In {{Year|1E 533}} during the reign of High King [[Lore:Wulfharth|Ysmir Wulfharth]], the Nordic Testing God [[Lore:Orkey|Orkey]] summoned an apparition of Alduin to help deal with the king. Despite his banishment, this ghost of Alduin Time-Eater was able to manifest and pose a threat. In this ghostly state, Alduin managed to eat the ages of the majority of the Nordic populace down to six years old, King Wulfharth included. Boy Wulfharth pleaded with the Dead God [[Lore:Shor|Shor]] who had fought Alduin previously in ancient times to help the Nords. Shor's own ghost then did battle with Alduin's in [[Lore:Sovngarde|Sovngarde]]. Shor defeated this shade of the Time-Eater, placing ruin on Orkey's chosen people, the [[Lore:Orc|Orcs]].{{ref|name=FSOKW|}}

Wulfharth, for his part, learned a new thu'um while watching the battle known as What Happens When You Shake the Dragon Just So. Wulfharth used this thu'um to restore the Nords back to their proper ages - inadvertently aging himself to death.{{ref|name=FSOKW|}} Some tellings forget Wulfharth's part in the tale, and claim that through unknown means, Shor alone restored the Nords back to their proper ages.{{ref|name=VOF}}

In {{Year|1E 2812}} Emperor Reman II permitted the construction of Alduin's Wall within [[Lore:Sky Haven Temple|Sky Haven Temple]].{{ref|name=AOTD|{{Cite Book|Annals of the Dragonguard}}}} It is considered to be one of the best preserved examples of early [[Lore:Akavir|Akaviri]] sculptural relief, as well as a wonder of the ancient world. Its purpose was to record the accumulated dragonlore and prophecy that the [[Lore:Dragonguard|Dragonguard]] possessed at the time.{{ref|name=SREsbern|}} Its main focus was a pictorial representation of the [[Lore:Prophecy of the Dragonborn|Prophecy of the Dragonborn]]. The prophecy foretold of five key historical events that would preface Alduin's return after his expulsion during the Dragon War.{{ref|name=TBotD|{{Cite Book|The Book of the Dragonborn}}}} Given that Alduin's return was inevitable, the Dragonguard believed that the wall was their gift to the generations that were to follow.{{ref|name=SREsbern}}

[[File:SR-quest-Alduin's Wall.jpg|Alduin's Wall|thumb|left]]
Craftsmen from temples across the [[Lore:Empire|Empire]] were called upon to work on the wall, while Grandmaster [[Lore:Jaiv-Yora|Jaiv-Yora]] of the Dragonguard oversaw the construction of the wall,{{ref|name=GJY|{{Cite Book|Grandmaster Jaiv-Yora (1E 2808 - 2835)}}}} and after six years it was completed. Emperor Reman II returned to the temple in {{Year|1E 2818}} to officially dedicate the wall. The Blood Seal outside the temple was consecrated in the presence of all Akaviri Dragonguard in [[Lore:Skyrim|Skyrim]]. This allowed the temple to be sealed, only ever to be opened by a Dragonborn to unlock its knowledge and power.{{ref|name=AOTD}}

[[File:ON-render-Akatosh vs. Alduin Crate.jpg|A clash between followers of Alduin and Akatosh|thumb|right]]
The presence of dragons caused distress for the Nords who viewed them as the servants of Alduin.{{ref|name=ESODragonRise|[[ON:Dragon|Dragon]] Sightings during the [[Online:Dragon Rise#Dragon Sightings|Dragon Rise]] event in [[Online:Online|ESO]]}} Some lighthearted level of respect could be shown for the World Eater in Nord culture, usually manifesting in small ways such as naming one's pet after him.{{ref|name=ONAW|[[Online:Alduin (wolf)|Alduin]] in [[Online:Online|ESO]]}}

Nords who viewed Alduin as their local variation of Akatosh were perplexed by the [[Lore:Imperial|Imperial]] fascination with Akatosh, as they worked diligently to keep the Time Dragon asleep while their southern cousins sought his attention.{{ref|name=DATN|}} Those who did not view Alduin as an aspect of Akatosh instead viewed Akatosh as the Great Dragon and Dragon totem deity.{{ref|name=DC|}}{{ref|name=TAAD|}} Some even called Alduin's godhood into question, placing him below Akatosh in the order of the cosmos.{{ref|name=AIR|}}{{ref|name=TAAD|}} Regardless, none denied his role as the harbinger of the End Times. Some outsiders who did not understand this dynamic of acknowledgment and respect, but lack of worship for the World Eater in Nordic culture claim that Nords worshipped the "heathen god" Alduin.{{Ref|name=JD|[[Bloodmoon:Jeleen|Jeleen]]'s dialogue in ''[[Morrowind:Morrowind|Morrowind]]: [[Bloodmoon:Bloodmoon|Bloodmoon]]''}}

In the [[Lore:Fourth Era|Fourth Era]], other prophetic deductions formed around Alduin's return. The revived [[Lore:Mythic Dawn|Mythic Dawn Cult]] leader [[Lore:Vonos|Vonos]] had visions which eventually led to the realization that Alduin would re-emerge into the world, the fated Last Dragonborn would rise, and that the two beings would clash despite both being shards of Akatosh's soul.{{ref|name=VonosJournal}}{{NewLeft}}

===Return===
[[File:LG-cardart-Alduin Alt.png|Alduin in Helgen|thumb|left]]
[[File:SR-quest-Alduin's Bane 02.jpg|Alduin does battle with Paarthurnax|thumb|right]]
[[File:SR-quest-Dragonslayer.jpg|Alduin raining down fury during his final stand in Sovngarde|thumb|right]]
In {{Year|4E 201}}, Alduin [[Lore:Return of the Dragons|reappeared]] at the [[Lore:Throat of the World|Throat of the World]], cast forth from the Time-Wound created when he had been banished thousands of years before. Perched here was his brother, Paarthurnax, who had awaited his return at the summit of the Throat of the World ever since his banishment.{{ref|name=Paarthurnax|}} However, Paarthurnax was unable to contain Alduin, and the World-Eater descended upon the region. In the midst of all of this, several members of the [[Lore:Stormcloaks|Stormcloak rebellion]] were being executed by Imperial Legionnaires in [[Lore:Helgen|Helgen]], including the rebel leader [[Lore:Ulfric Stormcloak|Ulfric Stormcloak]].{{ref|name=SRUnbound|[[SR:Unbound|Unbound]] quest in ''[[SR:Skyrim|Skyrim]]''}} Accidentally caught up in this string of executions was a [[Lore:Hero|prisoner]] who was detained for crossing the border.{{ref|name=SRUnbound|}}

Before this prisoner or Ulfric Stormcloak could be executed, Alduin descended upon Helgen and quickly reduced the settlement to rubble,{{ref|name=SRUnbound}} before flying away to [[Lore:Skuldafn|Skuldafn]].{{Ref|name=SkuldafnNotes|[[Skyrim:Skuldafn#Notes|Alduin seen flying to Skuldafn]] after Unbound in ''[[SR:Skyrim|Skyrim]]''}} Several parties were able to narrowly escape the destruction including Ulfric and the prisoner.{{ref|name=SRUnbound}} Ulfric Stormcloak's resulting escape prolonged the civil war in Skyrim and secured a steady flow of souls for Alduin to consume in Sovngarde.{{ref|name=SRCivil|[[SR:Civil War|Civil War]] questline in ''[[SR:Skyrim|Skyrim]]''}} Alduin began to resurrect his fallen brethren, using a powerful thu'um (seemingly known only to him) to undo their deaths and restore flesh to their bones.{{ref|name=SRBlade|[[Skyrim:A Blade in the Dark|A Blade in the Dark]] quest in ''[[Skyrim:Skyrim|Skyrim]]''}} Dragon Priests and [[Lore:Draugr|draugr]] began to stir in ancient ruins as well, setting the revitalization of Alduin's army and the Dragon Cult in motion. The mysterious prisoner was eventually revealed to be the [[Lore:Last Dragonborn|Last Dragonborn]], hero of prophecy.{{ref|name=SRDragonRising|[[Skyrim:Dragon Rising|Dragon Rising]] quest in ''[[Skyrim:Skyrim|Skyrim]]''}}

As a Dragonborn, the hero was able to wield the thu'um with preternatural ease, learning several powerful shouts that allowed them to slay Alduin's dragon allies and absorb their souls, increasing the hero's strength and ensuring Alduin could not revive them again. With the aid of an Elder Scroll, the Last Dragonborn learned the forgotten thu'um known as Dragonrend that served as a great weapon against dragonkind, forcing understanding of mortality into their immortal minds.{{ref|name=SkyrimAB}} With this new magic, Paarthurnax and the hero confronted Alduin on the Throat of the World, where the two sides fought to a stalemate. Given his unique nature, it was seemingly impossible to slay Alduin on Mundus.{{ref|name=SkyrimAB}}

Eventually, the Dragonborn gained access to Sovngarde, where Alduin had fled to regain strength.{{ref|name=SkyrimDragonslayer|[[Skyrim:Dragonslayer|Dragonslayer]] quest in ''[[Skyrim:Skyrim|Skyrim]]''}} Shor gave council for the heroes of Sovngarde to stand down, allowing the Last Dragonborn to face Alduin as foretold with the help of the three ancient Nord heroes who banished Alduin in the first place.{{ref|name=SRYsgramor|[[SR:Ysgramor|Ysgramor]]'s dialogue in ''[[Skyrim:Skyrim|Skyrim]]''}} With that, the quartet finally vanquished the World-Eater.{{ref|name=SkyrimDragonslayer|}} Alduin's soul was not absorbed by the Dragonborn, and he may not have been completely destroyed;{{ref|name=SkyrimDragonslayer|}} it is possible that he will one day return to fulfill his role as the World-Eater.{{ref|name=Arngeir|}}{{Ref|name=TWaS}}

The surviving dragons had varying reactions to Alduin's vanquishing. Paarthurnax lamented the death of his brother but intended to teach the surviving dragons the philosophy he had come to adopt, the [[Lore:Way of the Voice|Way of the Voice]].{{ref|name=Paarthurnax|}} Some dragons viewed this attempt at new leadership by Paarthurnax as tyranny, instead bowing to the superiority of the Last Dragonborn, who, as the slayer of Alduin, they felt was the rightful heir to his lordship.{{ref|name=Odahviing|[[Skyrim:Odahviing|Odahviing]]'s dialogue in ''[[Skyrim:Skyrim|Skyrim]]''}}

Even realms beyond the Mundus felt ripples from the World-Eater's destruction. A dragon in the [[Lore:Soul Cairn|Soul Cairn]] by the name of [[Lore:Durnehviir|Durnehviir]] learned of the event and proclaimed the Last Dragonborn a true Dovah for the deed.{{ref|name=DBDurnehviir|[[Skyrim:Durnehviir (dragon)|Durnehviir]]'s dialogue in ''[[Skyrim:Skyrim|Skyrim]]: [[Skyrim:Dawnguard|Dawnguard]]''}} Similarly, Miraak, the First Dragonborn in history who was residing in [[Lore:Apocrypha|Apocrypha]], sensed that the Last Dragonborn had slain Alduin and commended them for it.{{ref|name=Miraak|}}
